Do not rebuild the entire landing page without inspecting the current version.

16.1 Explain the product clearly

The landing page should quickly communicate what Zeno can actually do.

Potential messaging themes:

- AI-powered learning.
- Research assistance.
- Document workflows.
- Knowledge organization.
- Productivity tools.

Only make claims supported by the deployed application.

Do not claim unlimited usage, unrestricted model access, guaranteed accuracy, or premium capabilities unless these claims are technically and commercially accurate.

16.2 Demonstrate real capabilities

Use genuine screenshots or actual application output.

Where a live demonstration is feasible, make it functional.

Do not use fake conversations or fabricated testimonials.

16.3 Clear calls to action

Provide a clear path to:

- Start using Zeno.
- Try an available feature.
- Explore public resources.
- Open a shared artifact.

Do not create unnecessary signup walls for public content that is intentionally designed to be accessible without an account.

16.4 Onboarding

Provide a lightweight introduction to relevant features.

Avoid forcing users through a long tutorial.

Allow users to skip onboarding.

Do not request notification permission immediately on the first page load.

Request permissions only when the user has context for why they would be useful.

Acceptance criteria

- New visitors can understand the product.
- Main actions lead to working destinations.
- Messaging matches actual capabilities.
- Onboarding can be dismissed.
- Public pages remain accessible as intended.

