22. ENGINEERING RULES

Follow these rules throughout implementation.

Rule 1: Understand before modifying

Inspect the relevant files before editing them.

Do not blindly replace entire components.

Rule 2: Preserve existing behavior

Modify the smallest reasonable set of files.

Avoid unrelated refactoring.

Rule 3: Reuse existing abstractions

If Zeno already has a service, component, store, or utility for the required task, reuse it.

Do not create competing implementations.

Rule 4: No fake functionality

Every button, menu, form, and action must work or be explicitly disabled with a clear explanation.

Rule 5: No silent failure

Handle errors properly and provide useful feedback.

Log appropriate diagnostic information without exposing secrets.

Rule 6: Avoid unnecessary dependencies

Use the current framework and libraries wherever possible.

Add a dependency only when it provides a clear benefit.

Rule 7: Keep costs under control

Avoid expensive AI calls, excessive database reads, and unnecessary background jobs.

Use deterministic logic when it is sufficient.

Rule 8: Respect user privacy

Public sharing must always be deliberate.

Do not expose private work through discovery, analytics, profiles, or notifications.

Rule 9: Verify before declaring success

A successful build does not prove every feature works.

Test the actual critical workflows.

Rule 10: Be honest about limitations

If a feature requires unavailable infrastructure, credentials, subscriptions, or manual configuration, document the dependency.

Do not simulate success.

